INT. UNDERGROUND CURLING CLUB - SHEET THREE - NIGHT

A converted subway platform. Tile walls, strip lights, a stripe of imported ice laid down over concrete. Condensation drips from the ceiling in a slow clock. The air smells of coffee and old wool.

Four players and a referee stand at the hog line, brooms in hand.

NIKOLAI "KOLYA" ARSENIEV (50s, skip, cardigan, spine like a steel rod) crouches at the house, tapping the ice with his broom.

PADMA IYENGAR (30s, lead, ponytail, scrappy) is on the far hack, stone ready.

TEODORO "TEDDY" BRAVO (40s, second, sweaty, gold chain on top of his jersey) hovers behind her, nervous as a bridegroom.

JUNE ASHBY-LANE (20s, vice, lean, focused in a way that seems to cost her) holds her broom like a rifle.

Off to the side, REFEREE OKONKWO (60s, whistle, immaculate blazer) writes on a clipboard.

KOLYA
Padma. Draw to the button. Light.

PADMA
Light, Kolya.

KOLYA
Don't throw it like it owes you money.

PADMA
It does owe me money.

TEDDY
It owes all of us, Padma. Throw the thing.

She slides. The stone leaves her hand with a long grinding hum. Kolya and June sweep in step, their brooms hissing. The stone rolls, curls, drifts into the rings.

JUNE
Hard! Hard! Hard!

TEDDY
Off, off, it's fine, let it go!

The stone dies. Sits on the edge of the button. Perfect.

Padma rises out of her slide, grinning. Teddy claps his hands once, then covers his mouth, as if he'd been caught laughing in church.

OKONKWO
(not looking up)
Measure.

He kneels and lays a plastic gauge across the stone. A hush. Drops of condensation tick on the ice.

KOLYA
(quietly)
That is the match.

OKONKWO
Eleven millimetres to the opposition.

Kolya exhales. Teddy turns in a circle. June does not smile. She stares at her teammates and the smile she can't produce seems to push at the corners of her eyes.

JUNE
Teddy.

TEDDY
What?

JUNE
You touched the stone.

Everyone stops.

TEDDY
I didn't.

JUNE
Your sleeve. Your sleeve brushed the stone as it passed.

TEDDY
Kolya, tell her.

KOLYA
Teodoro.

TEDDY
(high)
It was the draught!

OKONKWO
(rising, clipboard lifted)
Sheet three, burned stone. It comes off.

PADMA
You cannot be serious.

^JUNE
Then take it off.

They speak together, flat and angry, over each other. Okonkwo does not blink. He picks the stone up with both hands and carries it off the ice.

Kolya takes off his glasses. Folds them. Slips them into the cardigan pocket as though he were closing a funeral.

KOLYA
Five years of Thursdays.

He turns and walks the length of the sheet. The brooms fall one by one behind him. Teddy's is the last, and it makes the loudest sound.

FADE OUT.
